<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
<!--
  THE TWO-FIELD SHAPE, and the second field is the whole point.

  A single field would force one of two bad choices. DeveloperControlled: an upgrade
  overwrites whatever the customer wrote, silently. SubscriberControlled: we can never ship a
  correction, because the customer's value — including the one they never touched — wins
  forever and we cannot tell an intentional edit from an untouched default.

  Two fields dissolve it. The default is ours and upgradeable; the override is theirs and
  untouchable by us. AND "has this org customised this question" stops being an inference
  and becomes a query: AAO_Org_Override__c != null.

  This survives upgrade BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by relying on manageability semantics we
  cannot test from a subscriber org — which is why the shape matters more here than it would
  if the upgrade behaviour were verifiable. See the journal, session 49.
-->
    <fullName>AAO_Shipped_Text__c</fullName>
    <description>OUR text, shipped with the package. Byte-exact as authored. DeveloperControlled, so an upgrade replaces it and a subscriber cannot edit it — which is what makes it a dependable default rather than a value that drifts silently per org.</description>
    <label>Shipped Text</label>
    <type>LongTextArea</type>
    <length>1024</length>
    <visibleLines>3</visibleLines>
    <fieldManageability>DeveloperControlled</fieldManageability>
</CustomField>
